<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Xipe Totec</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Xipe Totec, 'Our Lord the Flayed One', was a Mesoamerican god of spring, agricultural renewal and goldsmiths, worshipped by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a> and other peoples. In his festival of Tlacaxipehualiztli, priests wore the flayed skins of sacrificial victims, symbolising new growth bursting from the old husk. The Aztecs believed his cult came from the Yopes of the Pacific coast and called him the Yopi god.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xipe-totec.jpg" alt="Xipe Totec" width="320" height="361" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Katepanomegas · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xipe-totec.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The origin of Xipe Totec is uncertain. He was widely worshipped in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> at the time of the Spanish Conquest and was known through most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>; representations have been found as far away as Tazumal in El Salvador. His worship was common along the Gulf Coast in the Early Postclassic, and he probably became an important Aztec god after the Aztec conquest of the Gulf Coast in the middle of the fifteenth century.</p><p>The name joins xīpe, flayed, with totēc, the first-person plural possessive of tēuctli, lord. He was also called Tlatlauhqui Tezcatlipoca, Red Smoking Mirror, Yohuallahuan, the Night Drinker, and Yaotzin, revered enemy. The Tlaxcaltecs and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huexotzinca">Huexotzincas</a> worshipped a version of him as Camaxtli. In January 2019 archaeologists of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>'s National Institute of Anthropology and History confirmed the first known surviving temple of the god, in Puebla, built by the Popoluca between 1000 and 1260 CE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>In Aztec belief Xipe Totec was a god of life, death and rebirth, of agriculture, vegetation, the east, spring, goldsmiths and silversmiths, and of warfare, which he was believed to have invented. He was said to have flayed himself to feed humanity, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> seeds lose their outer layer before germinating and snakes shed their skin.</p><p>His insignia, the pointed cap and rattle staff, were the war attire of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> emperor, and his temple, the Yopico, stood within the Great Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>. He was also linked to pimples, inflammations, eye diseases and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a>, and offerings were made to him by the sick. He is shown wearing flayed human skin, often painted yellow on one side, with the skin of the hands hanging loose from the wrists.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>His festival, Tlacaxipehualiztli, literally the flaying of men, was held at the spring equinox before the rains, in March at the time of the Spanish Conquest. Its central act was the gladiatorial sacrifice of war prisoners, in which a captive tied to a large round stone fought an armed Aztec warrior.</p><p>The bodies were flayed, and priests and warriors wore the skins for twenty days, an act called Neteotquiliztli, the impersonation of a god. Wearers went through the city begging alms and blessing those who gave. People also touched the skins to relieve illness. Afterwards the skins were stored in lidded containers in a chamber beneath the temple. Goldsmiths held their own feast of Yopico in the same month.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Xipe Totec” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>There is some evidence that gods of other peoples were worshipped too, such as the Feathered Serpent of Teotihuacan and the Nahuatl god <b>Xipe Totec</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/zapotec-civilisation">Zapotec civilisation · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>The Aztecs associated the Yopes with the god <b>Xipe Totec</b>, known as the Yopi god, whose priests wore the flayed skins of sacrificial victims.</blockquote><a href="/state/yopitzinco">Yopitzinco</a></li></ul></section>
